\f0\b \cf0 \strokec2 What's left to build
\f1\b0 \

\f0\b 1. Input validation and error handling in the API
\f1\b0  Right now the API assumes clean input. It needs Pydantic models that validate every field in the incoming request and return useful error messages when something is missing or malformed.\

\f0\b 2. A test suite
\f1\b0  There are no automated tests yet. The right approach is 
\f3\fs26 pytest
\f1\fs24  with 
\f3\fs26 httpx.AsyncClient
\f1\fs24  to hit the API endpoints, plus unit tests directly on 
\f3\fs26 evaluate()
\f1\fs24  covering known edge cases \'97 the AP Grade 9 block, study hall triggers, biannual courses, and the AP US History grade-conditional logic.\

\f0\b 3. A front-end
\f1\b0  The API has no UI. The intended front-end is a web app (HTML/CSS/JS or React) with a student intake form and transcript upload. It would POST to the API and render the returned course plan as a readable multi-year schedule.\

\f0\b 4. Authentication
\f1\b0  If this were deployed for real use, the API would need some form of authentication so only authorized users (counselors, students) can access it \'97 at minimum an API key header check.\

\f0\b 5. Deployment
\f1\b0  Currently runs locally with 
\f3\fs26 uvicorn
\f1\fs24 . For real use it would need to be hosted \'97 options include a simple cloud VM, a platform like Render or Railway, or a serverless deployment. The 
\f3\fs26 .env
\f1\fs24  pattern already supports this cleanly.\
